You can use the `sort` method to sort the array by the `id` property and then use the `reduce` method to count the occurrences of each unique `id`. Here's how you can do it:

```typescript
function sortAndCountById(items: any[]): [number, number] {
    // Sort the array by the 'id' property
    items.sort((a, b) => a.id - b.id);

    // Use reduce to count the occurrences of each unique 'id'
    const count = items.reduce((acc, item) => {
        acc[item.id] = (acc[item.id] || 0) + 1;
        return acc;
    }, {} as Record<number, number>);

    return [count.length, count];
}

// Example usage:
const items = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Item 1' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Item 2' },
    { id: 1, name: 'Item 3' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Item 4' }
];

const [count, total] = sortAndCountById(items);
console.log(`Total items: ${total}, Unique items: ${count}`);
```

In this example, the `sortAndCountById` function sorts the array by the `id` property and then uses the `reduce` method to count the occurrences of each unique `id`. The result is a tuple where the first element is the total number of items and the second element is an object with the count of each unique `id`.
